{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791661468,"nonce":"VLXmoO3/y0caoQtwx2xrLR/63ZM=","title":"Image resampling runs on the render path","message":"Measured while answering whether images cost much in TypeScript (2026-10-10, a loaded machine, release addon): the TypeScript work is not the cost. Resolving 100 image records each frame, with their atlas rects cached, costs the same as 100 plain boxes in the renderer (about 0.13 ms), and sniffing and sizing one SVG took 10 us before that moved into Rust. What costs is native work done synchronously inside SceneRenderer.encode: resampling a 2048 by 1536 source took 6.6 ms to 400 by 300 and 34 ms to 1600 by 1200, each the first time an image is drawn at a size, and again at each new size bucket while a zoom or a resize runs. A frame that needs a new bucket can miss its deadline. Decoding is off the main thread now (native.loadImage); resampling is not.\n\nOptions: resample on a worker and draw the nearest bucket already in the atlas meanwhile, swapping in the new one when it lands; build a mip chain once per image so each bucket resamples from the next size up; make the resampler itself cheaper (it is about 18 ns a pixel). The first two together bound a hitch to nothing but a slightly soft image for a frame or two.","files":null}]}